#X text 660 20 bridge_analyzeReader: file-I/O/batch half of analyze_reader.js (startAnalysis \, startStem <n> \, resetMemory \, loadRegistry \, prepareNextTrack \, stemDone -- see analyze_reader_bridge.js's DISPATCH table). Inlet 0 takes those messages. [pd packOSC] -> [netsend -u -b] outbound \, same proven idiom as every other bridge in this project., f 380;
#X text 660 260 8 outlets \, matching the original js object's outlet numbering 1-8 (outlet 0 -- writes to slice_writer -- is NOT this object; that's analyze_reader.pd's direct patch cord \, already wired): outlet 1 = status (tag + args \, e.g. "reading vocals <path>" \, "all_done" \, "library N stems_done counter_set_to M") \, outlet 2 = counter advance (bang -- wire straight into [counter 1 4]'s bang inlet) \, outlet 3 = nDone (float) \, outlet 4 = counter set (already formatted as a 2-atom "set N" list -- wire straight into [counter 1 4]'s left inlet) \, outlets 5-8 = the file path to load for vocals/drums/bass/melody respectively (wire each into the matching stem_loader instance's inlet -- see stem_loader.pd)., f 380;
#X text 660 560 Send "stemDone" (bang) into inlet 0 whenever a stem's descriptor extraction genuinely finishes on the Pd side -- analyze_reader.pd's new outlet 1 does exactly this \, see CONVERSION_NOTES.md. That is what actually drives outlet 2's counter-advance now (the original js advanced its counter synchronously right after readStem() finished in-process; here the real analysis runs asynchronously in Pd\, so the bridge waits for this signal instead).;
#X text 660 640 Start the bridge alongside Pd: node bridge/analyze_reader_bridge.js --data-dir /path/to/EBYS/data --recv-port 9006 --send-port 9007 (distinct from the other three bridges' ports so all four can run at once).;
